      "trace": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of the numbers \\(3339\\), \\(2961\\), and \\(1491\\), we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm involves repeated division and taking remainders until we reach a remainder of zero. The last non-zero remainder is the GCD.\n\nFirst, let's find the GCD of \\(3339\\) and \\(2961\\).\n\n1. Divide \\(3339\\) by \\(2961\\) and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   3339 = 2961 \\cdot 1 + 378\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is \\(378\\).\n\n2. Now, apply the Euclidean algorithm to \\(2961\\) and \\(378\\):\n   \\[\n   2961 = 378 \\cdot 7 + 105\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is \\(105\\).\n\n3. Next, apply the Euclidean algorithm to \\(378\\) and \\(105\\):\n   \\[\n   378 = 105 \\cdot 3 + 63\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is \\(63\\).\n\n4. Then, apply the Euclidean algorithm to \\(105\\) and \\(63\\):\n   \\[\n   105 = 63 \\cdot 1 + 42\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is \\(42\\).\n\n5. Finally, apply the Euclidean algorithm to \\(63\\) and \\(42\\):\n   \\[\n   63 = 42 \\cdot 1 + 21\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is \\(21\\).\n\n6. Apply the Euclidean algorithm to \\(42\\) and \\(21\\):\n   \\[\n   42 = 21 \\cdot 2 + 0\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is \\(0\\).\n\nThe last non-zero remainder is \\(21\\). Therefore, the GCD of \\(3339\\) and \\(2961\\) is \\(21\\).\n\nNext, we need to find the GCD of \\(21\\) and \\(1491\\).\n\n1. Divide \\(1491\\) by \\(21\\) and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   1491 = 21 \\cdot 71 + 0\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is \\(0\\).\n\nSince the remainder is already \\(0\\), the GCD of \\(21\\) and \\(1491\\) is \\(21\\).\n\nThus, the greatest common divisor of \\(3339\\), \\(2961\\), and \\(1491\\) is \\(\\boxed{21}\\).",
